</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="header-tag">Strategic Analysis Report</div>
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A decade-long examination of pricing strategy and customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction genres on the Amazon bestseller list (2009–2019).</p>
    <div class="header-meta">
      <span><strong>Period:</strong> 2009–2019</span>
      <span><strong>Dataset:</strong> 550 records · 351 unique titles</span>
      <span><strong>Genres:</strong> Fiction &amp; Non-Fiction</span>
      <span><strong>Prepared:</strong> April 2026</span>
      <span><strong>Audience:</strong> Management</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 01</div>
    <div class="section-title">Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</div>
    <p class="section-desc">A high-level summary of the full decade: 50 bestselling books per year across 11 years, comprising 550 annual list entries from 351 distinct titles.</p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Total Records</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">550</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">50 titles × 11 years</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Unique Titles</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">351</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">199 multi-year appearances</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent2">
        <div class="kpi-label">Avg List Price</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$13.10</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Whole-dollar pricing</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card green">
        <div class="kpi-label">Avg User Rating</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">4.62</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Out of 5.0</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Avg Review Count</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">11,953</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Per annual list entry</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 32px;" class="two-col">
      <!-- Genre Split Donut -->
      <div class="chart-wrapper">
        <div class="chart-title">Genre Distribution</div>
        <div class="chart-subtitle">Annual list entries by genre, 2009–2019</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 32px;">
          <svg width="160" height="160" viewBox="0 0 160 160">
            <!-- Non Fiction: 310/550 = 56.36% = 203° arc -->
            <!-- Fiction: 240/550 = 43.64% = 157° arc -->
            <!-- Start at top (-90deg). Non Fiction first. -->
            <!-- NF arc: 0.5636 * 2π rad, Fiction: 0.4364 * 2π -->
            <circle cx="80" cy="80" r="60" fill="none" stroke="#e2e6ef" stroke-width="22"/>
            <!-- Non Fiction segment: stroke-dasharray = 56.36% of circumference=376.99 = 212.5, offset=0 -->
            <circle cx="80" cy="80" r="60" fill="none" stroke="#e85d26" stroke-width="22"
              stroke-dasharray="212.5 376.99"
              stroke-dashoffset="94.25"
              transform="rotate(-90 80 80)"/>
            <!-- Fiction segment: dasharray=164.5 376.99, offset = -(376.99-212.5) -->
            <circle cx="80" cy="80" r="60" fill="none" stroke="#2d6cdf" stroke-width="22"
              stroke-dasharray="164.5 376.99"
              stroke-dashoffset="-118.25"
              transform="rotate(-90 80 80)"/>
            <text x="80" y="76" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#1a2744">550</text>
            <text x="80" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6b7280">records</text>
          </svg>
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
              <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <span style="font-size:13px; font-weight:600; color:#e85d26;">● Non-Fiction</span>
                <span style="font-size:13px; font-weight:700;">310 <span style="color:#6b7280; font-weight:400;">(56.4%)</span></span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#e2e6ef; border-radius:4px; height:6px;">
                <div style="background:#e85d26; width:56.4%; height:100%; border-radius:4px;"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <span style="font-size:13px; font-weight:600; color:#2d6cdf;">● Fiction</span>
                <span style="font-size:13px; font-weight:700;">240 <span style="color:#6b7280; font-weight:400;">(43.6%)</span></span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#e2e6ef; border-radius:4px; height:6px;">
                <div style="background:#2d6cdf; width:43.6%; height:100%; border-radius:4px;"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- Rating Range summary -->
      <div class="chart-wrapper">
        <div class="chart-title">Rating Distribution Context</div>
        <div class="chart-subtitle">Bestsellers cluster in a narrow high-rating band</div>
        <div style="padding: 8px 0;">
          <div style="margin-bottom: 16px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted);">User ratings on this list span <strong style="color:var(--primary);">3.3 – 4.9</strong>, with the vast majority above 4.4. The narrow range means even small differences carry signal.</div>
          <!-- Mini histogram bars using rough data -->
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 6px; height: 80px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
            <div style="flex:1; background:#dbeafe; height:8%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="3.3-3.9: ~14"></div>
            <div style="flex:1; background:#93c5fd; height:22%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="4.0-4.2: ~42"></div>
            <div style="flex:1; background:#60a5fa; height:30%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="4.3-4.4: ~67"></div>
            <div style="flex:1; background:#3b82f6; height:100%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="4.5-4.6: ~175"></div>
            <div style="flex:1; background:#2563eb; height:88%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="4.7-4.8: ~165"></div>
            <div style="flex:1; background:#1d4ed8; height:42%; border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;" title="4.9: ~87"></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display: flex; gap: 6px; font-size: 10px; color: var(--muted);">
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">3.3–3.9</div>
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">4.0–4.2</div>
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">4.3–4.4</div>
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">4.5–4.6</div>
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">4.7–4.8</div>
            <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">4.9</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight">
      <strong>Key Context:</strong> With 36% of the 351 titles appearing in multiple years, a small set of perennial bestsellers drives recurring list presence. This repeat-appearance dynamic is central to understanding which pricing strategies sustain long-term visibility.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 02</div>
    <div class="section-title">Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</div>
    <p class="section-desc">Does paying more mean a better book? The data offers a clear and statistically grounded answer.</p>

