      <strong>Three Inflection Points:</strong><br><br>
      <strong>1. 2012 — Rating Trough:</strong> Average rating dipped to its decade low of 4.532, coinciding with the Fifty Shades trilogy and Fifty Shades of Grey's polarizing reception (3.8 stars). Viral bestseller status doesn't guarantee satisfaction.<br><br>
      <strong>2. 2015 — Pricing Reset (−28.8%):</strong> The sharpest single-year price drop in the dataset, from $14.64 to $10.42. This coincided with a surge of adult coloring books and budget-priced children's titles entering the list — genre composition drove the price floor, not a pricing strategy shift per se. Ratings held firm (4.648), proving the composition change was benign.<br><br>
      <strong>3. 2019 — Peak Rating (4.740):</strong> The decade closes with the best average rating and lowest average price ($10.08), alongside the highest review volume (15,898). The late-decade cohort — led by Where the Crawdads Sing (87,841 reviews), Becoming, and the Dog Man series — represents the most reader-validated bestseller class of the decade.
