<!-- SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
    <div class="section-title">Strategic Recommendations</div>
    <p class="section-desc">Every recommendation below traces directly to a specific finding in the data.</p>

    <div class="rec-grid">
      <div class="rec-card">
        <div class="rec-number">1</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Price Fiction in the $6–$10 Sweet Spot</div>
        <div class="rec-body">Fiction entries priced $6–$10 dominate the bestseller list (179 of 550 entries) and earn an avg rating of 4.65, while Fiction's overall avg price of $10.85 sits naturally in this band. Pricing above $15 provides no rating benefit — the correlation coefficient is −0.133.</div>
        <div class="rec-source">Data: Price bucket table · Fiction avg price · Pearson r</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card">
        <div class="rec-number">2</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Re-examine Non-Fiction Price Premium</div>
        <div class="rec-body">Non-Fiction titles average $14.84 — 37% above Fiction — yet earn a lower avg rating (4.595 vs. 4.648). The premium does not translate to perceived quality. The 2015 convergence year (when Non-Fiction prices fell to ~$11) achieved 4.645, the genre's strongest rating, at its lowest price point.</div>
        <div class="rec-source">Data: Genre comparison table · 2015 year data</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card">
        <div class="rec-number">3</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Invest in Series and Catalog Depth</div>
        <div class="rec-body">The top 10 authors account for 95 appearances through series franchises (Kinney, Riordan, Collins, Chapman). Series mechanics — reader loyalty, cross-sell, multi-year list tenure — are the dominant driver of sustained bestseller status. Catalog investment in serializable content yields compounding list presence.</div>
        <div class="rec-source">Data: Top 10 author appearances table</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card">
        <div class="rec-number">4</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Monitor Late-Decade Genre Composition Shift</div>
        <div class="rec-body">The 2019 cohort achieved the decade's highest avg rating (4.740) and lowest avg price ($10.08), driven by children's Fiction and personal-development Non-Fiction displacing higher-priced reference and political titles. Catalog strategy should track this compositional drift — it signals where consumer demand is migrating, not just a pricing trend.</div>
        <div class="rec-source">Data: YoY table 2019 · Price inflection analysis</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Summary scorecard -->
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:24px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a2744, #2d3f6c); color: white;">
      <div style="font-size:12px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:1.5px; text-transform:uppercase; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5); margin-bottom:16px;">Key Numbers to Take Into the Room</div>
